    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Assessment of the Offer\n\nAt first glance, this is a **strong initial offer** for an L6 Product Manager at Google in the Bay Area, but it has specific gaps you should address given your current compensation and strong leverage.\n\n#### Total Compensation Breakdown & The Vesting Structure\nGoogle does not vest initial RSUs equally over 4 years (25%/year). Google typically uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** (commonly 33% / 33% / 22% / 12% or 38% / 32% / 20% / 10%):\n\n| Year | Base | Target Bonus (20%) | RSUs (Assuming 33/33/22/12) | Total Comp (Excl. Refreshes) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | $235,000 | $47,000 | $356,400 (33%) | **~$638,400** |\n| **Year 2** | $235,000 | $47,000 | $356,400 (33%) | **~$638,400** |\n| **Year 3** | $235,000 | $47,000 | $237,600 (22%) | **~$519,600** |\n| **Year 4** | $235,000 | $47,000 | $129,600 (12%) | **~$411,600** |\n\n*Even if calculated at an even 25% split, annualized TC is **~$552,000/yr** vs. your current Meta TC of **~$526,000/yr**.*\n\n#### Pros\n* **Year 1\u20132 Cash Flow:** Front-loading delivers a substantial bump (~+$110k\u2013$160k) in your first two years compared to Meta.\n* **Equity Size:** A $1.08M initial grant for L6 PM is around the 75th percentile for Google initial bands.\n\n#### Cons / Red Flags\n* **Base Salary Decrease:** They offered $235,000 base, which is lower than your current $240,000 base at Meta and sits mid-band against the posted range of $183k\u2013$271k.\n* **$0 Sign-on Bonus:** Leaving a stable IC6 role at Meta where you are established and have $200k vesting in the next 12 months comes with transition risk; an offer with no sign-on bonus leaves money on the table.\n* **The Year 3\u20134 Cliff:** Because of front-loading, your compensation will drop in Years 3 and 4 unless you receive strong annual refreshers.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strategy: Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n**Do not accept as-is, and do not walk away yet. You should negotiate.**\n\nYou have the ideal negotiation posture:\n1. Google reached out to you (you were not actively looking).\n2. You are happy and performing well in an equivalent senior role (Meta IC6).\n3. The current offer represents only a modest steady-state increase once the initial front-loading normalizes.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Ask For and Specific Targets\n\nWhen negotiating with Google, recruiters typically have the most flexibility with **Sign-on Bonus** and **Equity (RSUs)**, followed by **Base Salary**.\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Negotiation Target | Justification |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$250,000 \u2013 $255,000** | You cannot take a base pay cut from your current $240,000; $255k is still within the posted $271k maximum. |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Offsets unvested equity/upcoming bonus timeline at Meta and covers first-year transition risk. |\n| **RSU Grant (4-yr)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,200,000 \u2013 $1,250,000** | Protects against the Year 3\u20134 vesting cliff and provides a true long-term premium over Meta. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. Framing & Script for the Recruiter\n\nFrame the conversation around being excited about the team and role, but clear that leaving a successful, happy role at Meta requires a package that is unequivocally compelling.\n\n> *\"I really enjoyed meeting the team and am genuinely excited about the problem space and scope of this L6 role. However, as I mentioned early on, I'm very happy and well-compensated at Meta with strong momentum.*\n> \n> *Looking at the numbers:*\n> 1. *The base of $235k is below my current base of $240k. Given the range up to $271k, I'd like to see the base salary at **$250k\u2013$255k**.*\n> 2. *To offset my upcoming unvested equity and make the move compelling, I\u2019d need a sign-on bonus of **$75k\u2013$100k**.*\n> 3. *To ensure my long-term compensation outpaces my trajectory at Meta beyond the initial two-year frontloaded window, I\u2019m targeting an initial equity grant of **$1.2M**.*\n> \n> *If we can get to these numbers, I\u2019d be thrilled to sign and join the team.\"*",
