     "content": "### The Bottom Line: **You should strongly consider accepting this offer.**\n\nFinancially, this is a **top-of-band package for an L6 Product Manager at Google**. Google effectively addressed your $200,000 walk-away equity concern by adding that entire amount directly to your grant ($1.08M $\\rightarrow$ $1.28M) and matching your $240k base salary. \n\nPushing further on compensation is not recommended\u2014$1.28M in initial equity is near the upper limit of Google's L6 PM compensation band. Pushing harder without an active competing offer risks exhausting goodwill.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Numbers: Why This Is a Win\n\nGoogle uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** (most commonly **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%**). Here is what your actual compensation trajectory looks like compared to your current Meta comp:\n\n| Year | Google Base | Google Bonus (20%) | Google RSUs ($1.28M grant) | **Google Total Comp** | Meta Total Comp | **Annual Delta** |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | $240,000 | $48,000 | $422,400 *(33%)* | **~$710,400** | ~$526,000 | **+$184,400** |\n| **Year 2** | $240,000 | $48,000 | $422,400 *(33%)* | **~$710,400** | ~$526,000 | **+$184,400** |\n| **Year 3** | $240,000 | $48,000 | $281,600 *(22%)* | **~$569,600\\*** | ~$526,000 | **+$43,600\\*** |\n| **Year 4** | $240,000 | $48,000 | $153,600 *(12%)* | **~$441,600\\*** | ~$526,000 | *Offset by Refreshes* |\n\n*\\*Years 3 and 4 do not yet include your annual Google equity refreshes, which start stacking into your compensation in Years 2 and 3.*\n\n#### Key Financial Insights:\n1. **You make up your \"lost\" Meta equity immediately:** You are gaining an extra **~$368,000+** across Years 1 and 2 compared to staying at Meta, which far exceeds the $200,000 in Meta stock you would walk away from.\n2. **Four-Year Guaranteed Baseline:** You are locking in **~$2.43M in baseline total comp** over 4 years before stock appreciation or refreshers.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Evaluating the Trade-Offs (Meta vs. Google)\n\nSince the money is clearly compelling, your decision comes down to scope, career trajectory, and day-to-day culture:\n\n| Factor | Meta (IC6) | Google (L6) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Culture & Pace** | High-velocity, scrappy, high accountability, bottom-up execution. | Consensus-driven, heavy stakeholder alignment, slightly slower release cycles. |\n| **Work-Life Balance** | Often intense, higher burnout risk, leaner teams post-\"efficiency\" era. | Generally better WLB and sustainability, though varies by team. |\n| **PM Autonomy** | PMs drive business metrics and ship fast; high product ownership. | Engineering-led culture; PMs must lead heavily via influence and consensus. |\n| **Career Risk** | **Low:** Established reputation, existing trust, predictable performance ratings. | **Moderate:** Need to rebuild your network, establish trust with new leadership, and ramp up on new infra/tooling. |\n\n---\n\n### 3. How to Decide\n\n* **Take the offer if:**\n  * You want to diversify your resume with both Tier-1 tech giants (having senior PM roles at both Meta and Google unlocks almost any executive/Head of Product role in tech).\n  * You want a massive **~$185k/year cash-flow jump** over the next two years.\n  * You like the specific Google team, product domain, and manager you'll be working under.\n\n* **Stay at Meta if:**\n  * You are on a clear, imminent track to **IC7 / Director-track** at Meta (an IC7 promotion within 12 months would outpace an L6 Google role long-term).\n  * You prefer fast, aggressive product execution over Google\u2019s consensus-oriented culture.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Next Step: How to Accept Gracefully\n\nIf you choose to accept, do so with enthusiasm:\n\n> *\"Thank you for working with the compensation committee to get the base matched and the equity grant updated to $1.28M. Given these updates, I\u2019m excited to accept the offer! Please send over the revised written agreement, and let\u2019s discuss the target start date.\"*",
